---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.url },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 2,
        name: "Privacy",
        item: `${site.url}/privacy`,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — Close My Lid"
  description="What Close My Lid collects, and what it never touches. No account, no telemetry, no server: the app flips one documented macOS power setting and nothing else."
  schema={schema}
>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main class="prose">
    <div class="proseTop">
      <p class="proseMeta">Last updated {site.privacyUpdated}</p>
      <a href="/" class="proseBack" data-astro-prefetch>← Back to home</a>
    </div>
    <h1 class="proseTitle">Privacy</h1>

    <p>
      Close My Lid is a free, MIT-licensed macOS menu bar app. It has no
      account, no server, and nothing to sign in to. This page explains what the
      app and this website touch, and what they never do.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>There is no account, no sign-in, and no licence check.</li>
      <li>
        The app collects no analytics, no telemetry, and no crash reports, and
        ships no third-party SDKs.
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing about your work leaves your Mac. Your session state lives in
        local preferences.
      </li>
      <li>
        The only network request the app makes is an update check against this
        project's own release feed.
      </li>
      <li>
        This website has no analytics, no cookies, and no advertising trackers.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The app</h2>
    <p>
      Close My Lid holds a Mac awake with the lid shut by setting <code
        >pmset -a disablesleep 1</code
      >, and restores normal sleep with <code>pmset -a disablesleep 0</code>.
      Those two commands are the only privileged thing it does. They are
      allowlisted, with no wildcards, in a <code>sudoers</code> drop-in at
      <code>/etc/sudoers.d/close-my-lid</code> that you approve once and can
      remove at any time from Settings › Administrator Access.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app asks macOS for no special permissions. There is no Accessibility,
      no Input Monitoring, no Screen Recording, and no keystroke watching. It
      does not read your source code, your prompts, your agent output, your
      terminal history, or your files.
    </p>

    <h2>Agent detection</h2>
    <p>
      The menu panel shows how many sessions of each supported coding agent are
      running. To do that it snapshots your own processes with a single
      <code>sysctl(KERN_PROC_UID)</code> call — it does not spawn <code>ps</code
      > or <code>pgrep</code>, and it cannot see other users' processes. It
      matches executable names, and for installs that run under a JavaScript
      runtime it reads the process arguments to find the install-directory path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those counts are computed on your Mac, displayed in the panel, and thrown
      away. They are never stored, never logged, and never transmitted.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored on your Mac</h2>
    <p>
      The app keeps your current session — the duration you picked and when it
      ends — plus your Launch at Login preference, in local user preferences. A
      heartbeat file lets the watchdog LaunchAgent release a stranded hold if
      the app dies. All of it stays on the machine, and uninstalling removes it.
    </p>

    <h2>Updates</h2>
    <p>
      The app checks for updates through Sparkle against a signed appcast served
      from this project's GitHub repository. That request carries your current
      app version and the ordinary network metadata any HTTP request carries,
      such as your IP address, which GitHub receives as the host. It does not
      carry an identifier for you, and there is no telemetry attached to it.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site runs no analytics, sets no cookies, and includes no advertising
      or tracking scripts. The Schibsted Grotesk typeface is served from this
      site rather than from Google Fonts, so loading a page makes no
      third-party request at all. Downloads are served from GitHub Releases, so
      GitHub sees those requests under its own privacy policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      There is nothing to request, correct, or delete, because no personal data
      is collected in the first place. If you contact the project through
      <a href={site.issues}>GitHub Issues</a>, whatever you write there is
      public and governed by GitHub's privacy policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      Close My Lid is a developer tool and is not directed at children under 13.
      No information is knowingly collected from them, or from anyone else.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If the way Close My Lid handles data ever changes, this page and the date
      at the top will be updated, and the change will be noted in the release
      notes.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about privacy: open an issue on
      <a href={site.repo}>GitHub</a> or reach out on
      <a href={site.twitter} rel="me noopener" target="_blank">
        {site.twitterHandle}
      </a>.
    </p>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
